winless NEWS -- history of user-visible changes

* Unreleased
  Over ssh: `sshw HOST` (or `winless ssh HOST`) is ssh with winless working on the host.  One ssh connection (ControlMaster) carries a private unix
  socket back to a relay on your desktop and, on demand, the port forward for each window; on the host `winless open` starts its server beside the file and
  rings the bell ($WINLESS_BELL) instead of launching a browser.  A host without winless gets a static build uploaded once (make builds remote/OS-MACHINE/winless;
  more platforms: a host with a C compiler builds one on first use and sshw keeps it in an archive, ~/.local/share/winless/remote/OS-MACHINE/).  New: sshw(1),
  winless --features.  Options about the shell (-A -X -t ...) go to the shell session, the others to the connection.
  Security: every server has a random secret (~/.cache/winless/ports/PORT, mode 0600); all requests but /ping must carry it (header, or a cookie obtained from
  /?t=SECRET, which is how the window is opened).  With it a forwarded port is accepted; without it nothing is, so other users of a shared host cannot run filters
  through your server.  `open` now prints "opened on port N" at once when its output is not a terminal.
  Revising with a model: mark text (n note, s slop, K keep), then `gr` hands the open notes to a model (claude -p by default; background job,
  Esc cancels) which answers with a list of edits.  winless verifies each edit (the text must occur exactly once and not touch a keep or
  another edit), shows the changes in place with word-level highlights, and you decide: n N y q c Y Enter Esc.  Finishing applies the
  accepted changes as one undo step, marks their notes done, and writes + commits the file if it was clean (never sweeping in your own
  uncommitted edits).  Commands: revise, review, backend; annotate --kind.  The API backend is not built yet.
  Annotations: `n` in visual mode saves a note on the selection in a SQLite database (file, time, begin and end row and column,
  the selected text, the note; several paragraphs store which ones, a whole-file note says so); notes are underlined or marked in the
  margin, listed with :annotations, visited with ]a / [a, hidden with :unannotate (never deleted).  Commands: annotate, annotations,
  unannotate.  Needs libsqlite3.
  Filters: :range!cmd, visual !, !! replace text with the output of a shell command (one undo step; nothing changes if it fails);
  '<> is exactly the selection.  Command: filter.
  The server refuses requests whose Host or Origin is not this machine's, so another website cannot make it edit, write or run a filter.
  Quitting a window now reaches the server again (a beacon, not a request that the closing window cancels).
  Every write keeps the old file (~/.local/share/winless/history) so any change can be reverted: `history`, `revert [N]`, and :history /
  :revert in the window.  A revert is itself a write, so it can be reverted; a write that cannot keep a copy is refused.
  One server per window: every `open` starts its own server on a port chosen at launch, so opening a window never changes another.
  New: `windows`, `open --new`, `open --here`, `stop --all`; a file already open is not opened twice; with several windows open a
  command asks which (a terminal) or lists them and exits 2.  A window's server ends with the window, as quitting vim does: at once
  on :q!, :wq or q, and a minute after the window is closed; unsaved edits are discarded then, and closing a window that has them
  asks first.  `open --here` and `stop` refuse while there are unsaved edits (--force discards them).
  Editing: an always-visible block cursor with vim motions (hjkl by screen row, w b e 0 $ ( ) { } gg G), v / V / mouse visual mode,
  d c y with motions and text objects (cw dd ciw cis dip x D C Y), counts, i a I A o O (typed in a bottom panel), r J p u Ctrl-r,
  :s substitute with ranges, and :w :q :wq.  New commands: redo, ex, type.  The server owns the text, keeps an
  undo stack, and writes atomically, refusing to overwrite a file that changed on disk.  New commands: edit, undo, write.
  The viewer reports its state right after every command (browsers throttle timers in a window that is not in front).
  Lines taller than the window are scrolled through row by row; scrolling is by screen rows throughout.
  Keys now follow vim: n/N (and the next/prev commands) wrap around at the ends of the file, h/l (or the arrow keys)
  scroll sideways when lines are chopped, Ctrl-d/u/f/b/e/y scroll, gg goes to the top. Help moved from h to F1 or :help.

* Version 0.2.0
  (This program was called winview until 2026-11-03; it is now winless -- "less" in its own Windows window.
  Renamed: the command, man page, WINVIEW_* environment variables (now WINLESS_*), and ~/.cache/winview (now
  ~/.cache/winless).)
  Rewritten in C (the 0.1.0 Python server and CLI are gone; viewer.html is unchanged apart from a page-up fix). One
  single-threaded poll() server, no dependencies beyond libc, builds with autoconf. Disconnected windows are noticed
  at once. The window opens full screen by default (--windowed for a normal one); it uses its own browser profile so that Edge
  honours the full-screen flag even when it is already running. Page up now exactly undoes page down.
  The tests are C programs in tests/ (unit tests of the parser and UTF-8 code, plus black-box tests that start the real
  binary); `make check` runs them, `make check-asan` runs them under AddressSanitizer/UBSan. No Python anywhere.

* Version 0.1.0
  First release (Python): a less-style viewer in its own Windows window (chromeless Edge/Chrome app window) driven from
  the shell through a local HTTP/SSE server.
